1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,540
December fifth, nineteen forty five.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:07,730
Five US Navy bombers take off from Florida on a routine training flight.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:16,960
Two hours later, the flight leader is on the radio, telling everyone his compasses have stopped working, and he has no idea where he is.

4
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:19,795
None of the five planes ever came back.

5
00:00:20,015 --> 00:00:22,669
Neither did the rescue plane sent to find them.

6
00:00:23,469 --> 00:00:28,131
And that, more or less, is how the Bermuda Triangle got its biggest legend.

7
00:00:29,531 --> 00:00:30,665
Hi, I'm Archie.

8
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:38,069
Welcome to The Quiet Archive, where we read the boring government paperwork so the spooky stuff has to earn its place.

9
00:00:38,569 --> 00:00:39,977
Today, Flight 19.

10
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:44,147
The case every Bermuda Triangle documentary opens with.

11
00:00:44,647 --> 00:00:49,380
So let's see what the Navy's own five hundred page report actually says.

12
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:50,317
Spoiler.

13
00:00:50,537 --> 00:00:51,857
It is not aliens.

14
00:00:53,257 --> 00:01:02,107
The planes were TBM Avengers, big single engine torpedo bombers, and the men flying them were nearly finished with their training.

15
00:01:02,607 --> 00:01:04,900
The leader was Lieutenant Charles Taylor.

16
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:06,033
Not a rookie.

17
00:01:06,253 --> 00:01:12,204
He had around two thousand five hundred flying hours and combat experience in the Pacific.

18
00:01:12,704 --> 00:01:16,155
The other four pilots were students on their last stretch of training.

19
00:01:16,375 --> 00:01:17,839
Fourteen men in total.

20
00:01:18,339 --> 00:01:21,425
The mission was called Navigation problem number one.

21
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:29,135
Fly east, practice bombing near a set of rocks, keep going east, turn north over the Bahamas, then turn back home.

22
00:01:29,635 --> 00:01:34,148
Taylor showed up late, and takeoff slipped to ten past two in the afternoon.

23
00:01:34,648 --> 00:01:35,617
Fun detail.

24
00:01:35,837 --> 00:01:37,573
The planes didn't have clocks.

25
00:01:37,793 --> 00:01:40,615
Everyone was expected to bring their own watch.

26
00:01:42,015 --> 00:01:43,776
The bombing practice went fine.

27
00:01:43,996 --> 00:01:47,672
The planes kept heading east, out over the open Atlantic.

28
00:01:48,172 --> 00:01:56,004
Then, sometime after three thirty, a radio operator on the ground picked up a strange conversation between the planes.

29
00:01:56,504 --> 00:02:00,379
Taylor was telling another pilot that both of his compasses were out.

30
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:06,844
Taylor looked down at the islands below him and decided he knew exactly where he was.

31
00:02:07,064 --> 00:02:08,321
The Florida Keys.

32
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:09,725
He did not.

33
00:02:09,945 --> 00:02:11,415
He was over the Bahamas.

34
00:02:11,915 --> 00:02:17,973
This is the navigation equivalent of thinking you're in your kitchen when you're actually in your neighbor's kitchen.

35
00:02:18,193 --> 00:02:19,327
Same appliances.

36
00:02:19,547 --> 00:02:20,826
Very different family.

37
00:02:22,226 --> 00:02:24,827
Here's why that one mistake was so dangerous.

38
00:02:25,047 --> 00:02:28,734
If you think you're over the Keys, then home is to the north east.

39
00:02:29,234 --> 00:02:31,267
So Taylor led the flight north east.

40
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:35,711
Which, from where they actually were, was straight out over the empty ocean.

41
00:02:36,211 --> 00:02:40,838
Every time he corrected course, he was correcting from the wrong starting point.

42
00:02:42,238 --> 00:02:45,673
On the ground, operators could hear the planes getting fainter.

43
00:02:46,173 --> 00:02:49,729
They asked Taylor to switch to the search and rescue radio channel.

44
00:02:49,949 --> 00:02:53,901
He refused, worried his planes would lose contact with each other.

45
00:02:54,401 --> 00:02:56,977
Some of the student pilots seem to have figured it out.

46
00:02:57,197 --> 00:03:01,533
One was heard saying, roughly, if we would just fly west, we would get home.

47
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:04,122
And for a while, they did turn west.

48
00:03:04,342 --> 00:03:09,042
Then Taylor decided they hadn't gone far enough east, and turned them around again.

49
00:03:09,942 --> 00:03:12,508
As the sun went down, the weather got worse.

50
00:03:12,728 --> 00:03:14,325
The sea below them was rough.

51
00:03:14,545 --> 00:03:16,574
And the fuel gauges kept dropping.

52
00:03:17,974 --> 00:03:26,694
Taylor's last messages were about keeping the planes together, and ditching all at once when the first plane got down to ten gallons of fuel.

53
00:03:27,194 --> 00:03:28,328
After that, nothing.

54
00:03:28,828 --> 00:03:36,016
Fourteen men, in five heavy planes, landing on a stormy sea, at night, somewhere off the coast of Florida.

55
00:03:37,416 --> 00:03:39,088
The Navy sent search planes.

56
00:03:39,308 --> 00:03:44,598
One of them was a big flying boat called a PBM Mariner, with thirteen men aboard.

57
00:03:45,098 --> 00:03:53,118
It took off at seven twenty seven that evening, made one routine radio call three minutes later, and was never heard from again.

58
00:03:53,618 --> 00:04:02,023
At about nine fifteen, the crew of a tanker, the SS Gaines Mills, saw flames shoot up from the sea and burn for around ten minutes.

59
00:04:02,243 --> 00:04:07,099
A nearby navy ship lost a plane from its radar at the same time and place.

60
00:04:07,599 --> 00:04:09,713
Mariners had a nickname among crews.

61
00:04:09,933 --> 00:04:11,231
Flying gas tanks.

62
00:04:11,451 --> 00:04:15,476
Their fuel lines were known to leak, and one spark could do the rest.

63
00:04:15,976 --> 00:04:20,122
So in one night, the Navy lost six aircraft and twenty seven men.

64
00:04:21,522 --> 00:04:24,671
The search covered hundreds of thousands of square miles.

65
00:04:24,891 --> 00:04:27,068
Ships, planes, and coast guard boats.

66
00:04:27,568 --> 00:04:28,505
No wreckage.

67
00:04:28,725 --> 00:04:29,912
No life rafts.

68
00:04:30,132 --> 00:04:30,810
Nothing.

69
00:04:32,210 --> 00:04:36,294
The Navy's investigation produced a five hundred page report.

70
00:04:36,794 --> 00:04:43,489
It concluded that Taylor had mistaken the Bahamas for the Florida Keys, and led the flight in the wrong direction.

71
00:04:43,989 --> 00:04:45,725
Taylor's mother pushed back hard.

72
00:04:45,945 --> 00:04:49,994
She argued her son was being blamed for something nobody could prove.

73
00:04:50,494 --> 00:04:55,614
And the Navy changed the official cause to, quote, causes or reasons unknown.

74
00:04:56,114 --> 00:04:58,949
Which is a perfectly fair thing to write in a report.

75
00:04:59,169 --> 00:05:03,306
And an absolutely perfect thing to write if you want to start a legend.

76
00:05:04,706 --> 00:05:06,911
Fast forward to nineteen sixty four.

77
00:05:07,131 --> 00:05:12,000
A writer named Vincent Gaddis publishes a magazine article and gives this patch of ocean a name.

78
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:14,693
The Bermuda Triangle.

79
00:05:15,193 --> 00:05:17,920
And Flight 19 becomes its poster child.

80
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:27,607
Books, documentaries, and even Close Encounters of the Third Kind, where the missing pilots step off an alien spaceship, looking great for their age.

81
00:05:28,107 --> 00:05:33,554
In real life, the men who appear in that movie don't even have the names of the real crew.

82
00:05:34,954 --> 00:05:37,270
So what does the evidence actually point to?

83
00:05:37,770 --> 00:05:41,947
A flight leader who got disoriented and was sure he was somewhere else.

84
00:05:42,447 --> 00:05:45,493
Compass problems, no clocks, and radio trouble.

85
00:05:45,993 --> 00:05:48,188
Bad weather, night falling, and a rough sea.

86
00:05:48,688 --> 00:05:56,531
And planes that simply ran out of fuel over deep water, where the Gulf Stream can carry wreckage a long way, very fast.

87
00:05:57,031 --> 00:06:00,559
Divers have found Avenger wrecks off Florida over the years.

88
00:06:00,779 --> 00:06:07,516
Every time someone checked the serial numbers, they turned out to be other planes, from other training accidents.

89
00:06:08,916 --> 00:06:10,642
So here's the honest version.

90
00:06:11,142 --> 00:06:12,079
Documented.

91
00:06:12,299 --> 00:06:16,153
Five Avengers and fourteen men were lost on a training flight.

92
00:06:16,373 --> 00:06:19,272
The leader believed he was over the Florida Keys.

93
00:06:19,492 --> 00:06:22,628
The flight ran low on fuel at night over rough seas.

94
00:06:22,848 --> 00:06:28,143
A rescue plane with thirteen men was lost, and a ship reported a fire on the water.

95
00:06:28,363 --> 00:06:30,494
The Navy listed the cause as unknown.

96
00:06:30,994 --> 00:06:31,724
Legend.

97
00:06:31,944 --> 00:06:39,205
Strange glowing water, mysterious messages, men taken by aliens, and a triangle that eats airplanes.

98
00:06:39,705 --> 00:06:42,306
What probably happened is quieter, and sadder.

99
00:06:42,526 --> 00:06:44,828
A long day, a wrong guess, and the ocean.

100
00:06:46,228 --> 00:06:47,407
So what do you think?

101
00:06:47,627 --> 00:06:51,286
Wrong turn, bad luck, or is there still something we don't know?

102
00:06:51,786 --> 00:06:53,030
Tell me in the comments.

103
00:06:53,250 --> 00:06:54,782
Please don't say aliens.

104
00:06:55,002 --> 00:06:55,648
Fine.

105
00:06:55,868 --> 00:06:57,200
You can say aliens.

106
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:58,021
Once.

107
00:06:58,521 --> 00:07:02,599
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

108
00:07:02,819 --> 00:07:07,201
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
